		<dc:description id="description">A collection of novellas and short stories by decadent New York author, Edgar Saltus.</dc:description>
		<meta id="long-description" property="se:long-description" refines="#description">
			&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/edgar-saltus"&gt;Edgar Saltus&lt;/a&gt; was a writer of decadent fiction with a pessimistic bent, as is common in fin de siècle literature, and characterized by a wit reminiscent of &lt;a href="https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/oscar-wilde"&gt;Oscar Wilde&lt;/a&gt; and other contemporaries. Between 1887, the year he debuted with &lt;i&gt;Mr. Incoul’s Misadventure&lt;/i&gt;, and 1895, novels, novellas, and short stories followed one another in rapid succession, and to great success. Critics condemned his works as immoral and the public was scandalized, but for a while, Edgar Saltus was infamous. His stories weave a common thread: love, lust and greed, distrust and deceit among the upper classes of late nineteenth-century New York, who also regularly retreat to Paris, the Riviera, London, and other destinations frequented by the super-rich of the time.&lt;/p&gt;
			&lt;p&gt;There are outliers. “A Transient Guest” finds its protagonist in the Dutch East-Indies. “Mary Magdalen,” an historical novella, recounts the final days of Jesus Christ through the perspective of Mary Magdalene. And although “Madam Sapphira” is more in line with his other works, its story bears striking similarities to the circumstances surrounding Saltus’s highly publicized divorce from his first wife. Written to clear his name and that of his corespondents, its publication had the opposite effect.&lt;/p&gt;
			&lt;p&gt;After 1895, the name Edgar Saltus began to lose its luster. He continued to write and publish, but less often and to much less attention. At the same time, the cynical worldview that defined his early output shifted. Inspired by Theosophy, he gradually became milder and more optimistic, the effect of which is noticeable throughout his later work.&lt;/p&gt;
			&lt;p&gt;During this second half of his career Saltus frequently revisited ideas, plot lines, phrases and passages. This was sometimes prompted by his newfound spirituality, but likely just as often he rewrote—as he once admitted with regret—earlier work to something new to sell to periodicals. For this collection, a careful editorial selection has been made to avoid overlap between individual stories in this collection, as well as overlap with other Edgar Saltus works on Standard Ebooks.&lt;/p&gt;
		</meta>
		<dc:language>en-US</dc:language>
